Evidence C - enrollment-service log:
14:04:12.481 INFO  POST /enrollments user=u_51207 course=postgres-performance
14:04:12.493 DEBUG tx COMMIT enrollment enr_88412 (12 ms)
14:04:17.498 WARN  publish enrollment.created failed for enr_88412: BrokerTimeoutError: publish to enrollments timed out after 5000 ms
14:04:17.499 INFO  POST /enrollments 201 Created 5018 ms
$ grep -c "WARN  publish enrollment.created failed" enrollment-service-2026-10-27.log
37      (first 14:03:13.115, last 14:05:54.310)

Evidence D - enrollment.service.ts:
const enrollment = await this.db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  await this.assertCanEnroll(tx, userId, courseId);
  return tx.enrollments.insert({ userId, courseId, status: "active" });
});                                                   // COMMIT
try {
  await this.broker.publish("enrollments", { id: randomUUID(), key: enrollment.id, type: "enrollment.created", payload: { ... } });
} catch (err) {
  this.logger.warn(`publish enrollment.created failed for ${enrollment.id}: ${err}`);
}
return enrollment;                                    // controller answers 201

Statement: "The commit and the publish are two independent writes; for 37 requests the publish failed after COMMIT had already made the enrollment durable, and the catch block turned each lost event into a 201."

Proposed fix in the incident channel:

return this.db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  await this.assertCanEnroll(tx, userId, courseId);
  const enrollment = await tx.enrollments.insert({ userId, courseId, status: "active" });
  await this.broker.publish("enrollments", toEvent(enrollment));   // throws -> rollback
  return enrollment;
});                                                                 // COMMIT

Statement: "With this change the enrollment and the event are atomic: a failed publish rolls the enrollment back, and a failed COMMIT withdraws the event that was already published."

Proposed fix after the review:

-- request
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO enrollments (...) VALUES (...);
INSERT INTO outbox (id, aggregate_type, aggregate_id, event_type, payload, status) VALUES ($4, 'enrollment', $1, 'enrollment.created', $5, 'pending');
COMMIT;

-- relay, every second
SELECT id, aggregate_id, event_type, payload FROM outbox WHERE status = 'pending' ORDER BY id LIMIT 100;
-- for each row: publish with message id = outbox.id, then
UPDATE outbox SET status = 'sent', sent_at = now() WHERE id = $1;

Statement: "Because the outbox row commits together with the enrollment, the relay publishes every event exactly once, so the certificate service can stop deduplicating by event id."
